prompt:
Now we are merging two PDDL domains into one domain. Please help merge two PDDL predicates.

predicate in domain_1: (eggs_shot ?e - entity) ; entity ?e is in the state 'eggs shot'; used as effect of shoot_eggs, precondition of sew_eggs
predicate in domain_2: (has ?e - entity ?i - item) ; relation 'has' holds; arguments: ?e is a entity, ?i is a item; used as precondition of drop_pebble_on_path, effect of drop_pebble_on_path, precondition of drop_breadcrumb_on_path, effect of drop_breadcrumb_on_path, effect of enter_witch_house, effect of collect_treasure

Please judge whether the two predicates can be merged into one predicate based on following rules:
(1) If the two predicates have the same name, they MUST be merged into one predicate;
(2) If the two predicates are semantically equivalent, they MUST be merged into one predicate;
    but not too general or complex, such as "(cup ?c): c is a cup" and "(mug ?m): m is a mug" can be merged into one predicate "(cup ?c): c is a cup" because they are both "cup" in semantics, but not "(container ?o): o is a container" because the semantics are extended too much. 
(3) It the two predicates are containing relationship, do NOT merge them in case of complexity in PDDL operators;
    such as "(on ?x ?y) ; x is on top of y" and "(ontable ?x ?y), x is on the table y". Although "ontable" is a subset of "on", it is not recommended to merge them into one predicate.
    Namely, do NOT generalize the two predicates into one predicate if the logic becomes more complex after merging.

Your output should include three part:
(1) reasoning: your analysis process;
(2) merge_flag: whether the two predicates can be merged into one;
(3) new_predicate: if equivalent, output the new predicate in PDDL format (in one of the given predicates or a new invented predicate).

If "merge_flag" is false, the "new_predicate" part is null.

Your output should be in JSON format like below:
{
    "reasoning": "your analysis process",
    "merge_flag": true,
    "new_predicate": ["(new_predicate ?param)", "A brief summary of the new predicate like the two given predicates."]
}

Do NOT output any strange symbols like "\".
